### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants a simple filter operation that uses `String.startsWith` as the predicate. This tests basic stream filtering and string method knowledge.

### How do you filter employees whose name starts with A?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class NameStartsWithA {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        List<Employee> result = employees.stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getName().startsWith("A"))
            .toList();

        result.forEach(e ->
            System.out.println(e.getName() + " (SAV" + e.getAccountId()
                + ") - " + e.getDepartment()));
    }
}
```

```text
Amit (SAV2004) - RISK
Ananya (SAV2009) - COMPLIANCE
```

The predicate `e -> e.getName().startsWith("A")` returns true for names beginning with the capital letter A.

### Why is startsWith case-sensitive?

`startsWith` compares characters using their exact values. The capital letter A (Unicode 65) and the lowercase letter a (Unicode 97) are different characters.

```java
System.out.println("Amit".startsWith("A"));   // true
System.out.println("amit".startsWith("A"));   // false
System.out.println("AMIT".startsWith("A"));   // true
```

### How do you make it case-insensitive?

Normalize the case before comparing.

```java
// Case-insensitive: convert to uppercase first
List<Employee> caseInsensitive = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().toUpperCase().startsWith("A"))
    .toList();

// Alternative: use regionMatches with ignoreCase flag
List<Employee> regionMatch = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().regionMatches(true, 0, "A", 0, 1))
    .toList();
```

### How do you handle null names?

Real banking data often has null or blank names from incomplete records.

```java
// WRONG: NullPointerException on null names
List<Employee> wrong = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().startsWith("A"))  // NPE if name is null!
    .toList();

// CORRECT: null guard with short-circuit evaluation
List<Employee> correct = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName() != null && e.getName().startsWith("A"))
    .toList();

// CORRECT: using Optional
List<Employee> withOptional = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> Optional.ofNullable(e.getName())
        .map(n -> n.startsWith("A"))
        .orElse(false))
    .toList();
```

The null check comes first. Java's short-circuit `&&` operator skips the `startsWith` call when the name is null.

### How do you parameterize the prefix?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;

public class EmployeeFilterService {

    public static List<Employee> filterByNamePrefix(
            List<Employee> employees, String prefix) {
        return employees.stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getName() != null
                && e.getName().toUpperCase()
                    .startsWith(prefix.toUpperCase()))
            .toList();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        System.out.println("Names starting with 'R':");
        filterByNamePrefix(employees, "R").forEach(e ->
            System.out.println("  " + e.getName()));

        System.out.println("Names starting with 'Pr':");
        filterByNamePrefix(employees, "Pr").forEach(e ->
            System.out.println("  " + e.getName()));
    }
}
```

```text
Names starting with 'R':
  Rajesh
Names starting with 'Pr':
  Priya
```

### How is startsWith different from contains and matches?

| Method | Checks | Example | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| startsWith("A") | Beginning of string | "Amit".startsWith("A") | true |
| contains("mi") | Anywhere in string | "Amit".contains("mi") | true |
| endsWith("it") | End of string | "Amit".endsWith("it") | true |
| matches("A.*") | Entire string regex | "Amit".matches("A.*") | true |

```java
// startsWith: beginning only
List<Employee> prefix = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().startsWith("A")).toList();

// contains: anywhere in the name
List<Employee> anywhere = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().contains("a")).toList();

// matches: full regex pattern
List<Employee> regex = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().matches("[A-D].*")).toList();
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: forgetting case sensitivity.

```java
// WRONG: misses lowercase names
List<Employee> wrong = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().startsWith("A"))
    .toList();  // misses "amit" if data has lowercase names

// CORRECT: normalize case
List<Employee> correct = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getName().toUpperCase().startsWith("A"))
    .toList();
```

Mistake 2: using charAt instead of startsWith for multi-character prefixes.

```java
// WRONG for multi-character prefix: charAt only checks one character
boolean wrong = "Priya".charAt(0) == 'P';  // works for single char
boolean alsoWrong = "Priya".charAt(0) == 'P'
    && "Priya".charAt(1) == 'r';  // ugly, error-prone

// CORRECT: startsWith handles any prefix length
boolean correct = "Priya".startsWith("Pr");  // clean and readable
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you filter by the last character instead of the first?" Use `endsWith("a")` instead of `startsWith("A")`.
- "How do you group employees alphabetically by first letter?" Use `groupingBy(e -> e.getName().charAt(0))`.
- "What if you need multiple prefixes?" Create a `Set<String>` of prefixes and check `prefixes.stream().anyMatch(name::startsWith)`.
